345
00:43:03,170 --> 00:43:14,000
To conclude, Ed Dinner and Marty Seligman did very important research on extremely happy people.

346
00:43:14,080 --> 00:43:19,800
This is tip of the stem research. looking at the 10% of the happiest people out there,

347
00:43:20,260 --> 00:43:21,480
people that we can learn from.

348
00:43:22,080 --> 00:43:32,840
And what they found was very interesting. First of all, these people did not experience painful emotions any less times than the rest of the people,

349
00:43:33,200 --> 00:43:37,280
say the bottom 10% or the mid-10%.

350
00:43:37,900 --> 00:43:42,780
They experienced painful emotions. The difference between them and the rest was

351
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:54,060
that they recovered more promptly as a result of different interpretations. So when they felt down, they were optimists.

352
00:43:54,200 --> 00:43:57,360
Instead of saying, okay, so I'm going to feel down for the rest of the month,

353
00:43:57,800 --> 00:43:59,440
they said, okay, so I'm feeling down.

354
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:00,320
Big deal.

355
00:44:00,720 --> 00:44:10,860
What can I learn from it? What happened? How can I make myself feel better? And then they recovered more promptly than the pessimists, the people who remained depressed for a much longer time.

356
00:44:11,640 --> 00:44:15,500
You see, events happen.

357
00:44:15,880 --> 00:44:17,100
Things happen in the world.

358
00:44:18,140 --> 00:44:20,100
Very often out of our control.

