00:46:10,610 --> 00:46:28,430
Let me move on and talk about the question of focus.

376
00:46:31,420 --> 00:46:37,120
Because just like if we focus on specific element of an event or not makes a difference,

377
00:46:37,500 --> 00:46:43,260
it applies on the macro level. to our entire life. What I'm going to do now is elaborate,

378
00:46:43,720 --> 00:46:49,680
go at a higher level of the spiral of understanding and learning of something that we've talked about time and again.

379
00:46:52,840 --> 00:46:54,960
Ed Diner, who's also,

380
00:46:55,610 --> 00:47:02,900
whom I just mentioned his research, he's also the president, first president of the International Positive Psychology Association. He's from Illinois.

381
00:47:04,520 --> 00:47:14,300
After years of research, concludes, it appears that the way people perceive the world is much more important to happiness than objective circumstances.

382
00:47:15,220 --> 00:47:19,920
Now, if you think about it, that makes a lot of sense. We know a lot of people who seemingly have everything,

383
00:47:20,300 --> 00:47:24,460
whether it's wealth, whether it's wonderful friends and family,

384
00:47:25,200 --> 00:47:32,000
all their dreams have come true, and they're still miserable, or there are people who have very little, who have experienced hardship after hardship,

385
00:47:33,120 --> 00:47:43,460
and who never cease to celebrate life. Then there are people who have everything going for them and really appreciate it. don't take it for granted and are happy.

386
00:47:43,660 --> 00:47:48,080
And then there are people who don't have much and never cease to complain.

387
00:47:51,230 --> 00:47:56,390
In other words, it's not just the external that matters.

388
00:47:58,050 --> 00:48:04,850
What matters more is the internal. Happiness is not contingent on our status or the state of our bank account.

389
00:48:04,850 --> 00:48:16,770
It's much more dependent on our state of mind, on what we choose to focus on. Because our emotions are determined by external circumstances as well as internal.

390
00:48:18,430 --> 00:48:30,330
Interpretation matters. So for example, look at what Edison did. 5,000 failures to the external observer. For him, the evaluation was very different. The internal evaluation, the subjective evaluation.

391
00:48:30,330 --> 00:48:34,330
It was 5,000 times where I showed what doesn't work.

392
00:48:38,520 --> 00:48:48,790
And it's up to us to decide where we focus. Do we celebrate successes or take it for granted?

393
00:48:49,110 --> 00:48:55,430
Do we perceive failures and hardships as a disaster or as an opportunity for growth?

394
00:48:58,110 --> 00:49:02,830
Emerson, two different minds, the same world is a hell and a heaven.

395
00:49:03,470 --> 00:49:13,650
And Shakespeare, nothing is good or bad, but thinking makes it so. Now, I don't 100% agree with Shakespeare, but he is to a great extent right.

396
00:49:13,650 --> 00:49:17,450
A person in Darfur is living in hell.

397
00:49:18,570 --> 00:49:24,570
And it's very difficult to reframe and to derive and to look at the positive in that situation.

398
00:49:26,600 --> 00:49:29,700
Same for a person living in a concentration camp.

399
00:49:31,560 --> 00:49:41,700
So there are external circumstances that matter. A homeless person, if you give them enough money for a home, for food, for basic education,

400
00:49:41,700 --> 00:49:45,860
of course will become happier because of a change in external circumstances.

401
00:49:47,540 --> 00:49:58,880
However, beyond the basic needs, beyond the basic freedoms, it is mostly about how we perceive reality and we're able to make it into a heaven or hell.

402
00:50:01,380 --> 00:50:08,880
Let's do an exercise that will capture this idea of focus creating reality.

403
00:50:09,460 --> 00:50:11,220
So I have a question for you.

404
00:50:12,140 --> 00:50:14,080
How many geometric shapes?

405
00:50:15,920 --> 00:50:18,540
Unfortunately, it's a great exercise, but it only works once.

406
00:50:20,280 --> 00:50:30,140
remember this how you created your reality just based on the question that you asked we think when we look at a picture now everyone of course is looking at the children on the bus and the clock

407
00:50:30,980 --> 00:50:35,340
but before it was right in front of you and most of you did not see it

408
00:50:38,260 --> 00:50:47,600
because it is a question of focus very often we complain about life and we say things are terrible and awful not realizing that we're the cause of it

409
00:50:48,480 --> 00:50:56,060
either by imagining it and then beliefs become self-fulfilling prophecies or because we just focus on what is not working.

410
00:50:56,880 --> 00:51:06,960
And once again, this does not mean that there are no objective, terrible circumstances out there. But to a great extent, we co-create our reality.

411
00:51:07,940 --> 00:51:19,700
Now, one of the students who was in 1504 two years ago recommended this book to me, Way of the Peaceful Warrior. And it's a wonderful book. I just started reading it, and there's a movie on it, right?

412
00:51:19,800 --> 00:51:25,000
I just started reading it yesterday, and I came across this, coincidentally, this parable,

413
00:51:25,680 --> 00:51:33,100
so I want to read it to you. It's a story that Dan Millman, the author, tells.

414
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:40,410
I met him on a construction site in the Midwest.

415
00:51:41,670 --> 00:51:52,530
When the lunch whistle blew, all the workers would sit down together to eat, and every day Sam would open his lunch pail and start to complain.

416
00:51:53,770 --> 00:52:00,330
Son of a gun, he'd cry. Not peanut butter and jelly sandwich again. I hate peanut butter and jelly.

417
00:52:01,210 --> 00:52:05,850
He whined about his peanut butter and jelly sandwiches day after day after day

418
00:52:06,570 --> 00:52:10,250
until one of the guys on the work crew finally said,

419
00:52:11,050 --> 00:52:14,430
Sam, if you hate peanut butter and jelly so much,

420
00:52:15,030 --> 00:52:25,340
why don't you just tell your old lady to make you something different what do you mean my old lady Sam replied I'm not married I make my own sandwiches

421
00:52:28,580 --> 00:52:38,070
now you know very often we make our own sandwiches and we don't even notice it

422
00:52:38,070 --> 00:52:49,580
because the questions that we ask the questions that we ask determine our reality a peanut butter sandwich or geometric shapes or children on the bus

423
00:52:49,580 --> 00:52:55,280
or salami whatever it is we very often create our own reality.

424
00:52:58,330 --> 00:53:04,310
And if we understand this, we can transform, change the way that we perceive,

425
00:53:04,710 --> 00:53:07,410
change our focus, change our questions,

426
00:53:07,910 --> 00:53:13,750
whether it's questions disputing irrational evaluations, whether it's questions focusing on the positive.

427
00:53:14,270 --> 00:53:17,530
And the questions create a new reality.

428
00:53:20,780 --> 00:53:32,640
There are essentially two archetypes that I'd like to discuss here, Two archetypes that capture in many ways how we can cognitively reconstruct and hence create our reality.

429
00:53:33,460 --> 00:53:39,940
The two archetypes are the benefit finder and the fault finder.

430
00:53:40,940 --> 00:53:48,460
First of all, the fault finder. The fault finder is a person who always focuses on things that don't work.

431
00:53:48,460 --> 00:53:57,680
on the peanut butter and jelly, on things that are not going well, on problems in the relationship, on problems in him or herself,

432
00:53:57,860 --> 00:54:08,260
problems with the job, the consummate complainer. Now, we all exist on the continuum between extreme fault-finding and extreme benefit-finding.

433
00:54:09,560 --> 00:54:18,400
There's no one who is all the way on the extreme this way or that way. Everyone is somewhere on the continuum. and what I'm going to argue for is to move a little bit more

434
00:54:18,400 --> 00:54:25,840
from the fault finding toward the benefit finding because there are many benefits to it that I'll discuss later.

435
00:54:26,700 --> 00:54:34,640
The fault finder, I got the name from the work of Henry David Thoreau, the fault finder will find faults even in paradise.

436
00:54:37,330 --> 00:54:45,650
The extreme fault finder experiences resignation, learned helplessness.

437
00:54:46,710 --> 00:54:57,570
Why? Because he or she begins to really believe that the terrible reality is very much out there independent of what he or she thinks,

438
00:54:57,570 --> 00:55:00,010
not recognizing that they create that reality.

439
00:55:00,950 --> 00:55:05,610
They begin to believe that no matter what they do, no matter what job they find, they have a terrible boss.

440
00:55:06,570 --> 00:55:10,390
No matter what partner they have, they're always awful and inconsiderate.

441
00:55:12,620 --> 00:55:23,840
No matter what restaurant they go to, the service is always awful. and they always keep on getting the same peanut butter and jelly sandwich that they made for themselves.

442
00:55:23,880 --> 00:55:30,480
And they resigned to the reality and that becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy, of course, their existence.

443
00:55:32,160 --> 00:55:40,360
A fault finder makes lemonade out of lemons. Sorry, makes lemon out of lemonade.

444
00:55:43,360 --> 00:55:49,400
The benefit finder, research Julian Bauer, now at UCLA.

445
00:55:49,400 --> 00:55:57,350
The benefit finder is the opposite, focuses on what works.

446
00:55:57,790 --> 00:56:02,630
The benefit finder looks on the bright side of life,

447
00:56:04,520 --> 00:56:08,300
finds the silver lining in a dark cloud,

448
00:56:09,680 --> 00:56:12,460
makes lemonade out of lemons,

449
00:56:12,860 --> 00:56:16,940
and doesn't fault an instructor for using too many cliches.

450
00:56:22,350 --> 00:56:28,470
The benefit finder finds the miracle in the common.

451
00:56:29,970 --> 00:56:35,150
Ralph Waldo Emerson, a consummate benefit finder, focusing on the positive, on what works.

452
00:56:35,910 --> 00:56:45,070
Now, the problem here may be that the benefit finder may be detached. This is one of the criticisms of people who always see the bright side of life.

453
00:56:48,170 --> 00:56:50,790
And that is a problem, if that is the case.

454
00:56:51,150 --> 00:56:56,430
Again, this class is not advocating focusing only on the positive. It's advocating reality.

455
00:56:57,410 --> 00:57:03,930
And reality comprises both good and bad. Reality comprises both geometric shapes and children on the bus.

456
00:57:05,130 --> 00:57:15,910
And the key is to focus on both. So the detachment, the detached benefit finder, is certainly not an archetype that I would advocate, nor would it lead to mental health in the long run.

457
00:57:18,140 --> 00:57:22,240
There's a need, in the words of Nathaniel Brandon, to respect reality.

458
00:57:22,240 --> 00:57:35,310
We don't create reality simply by the focus, however we are co-creators.

459
00:57:35,370 --> 00:57:38,050
Let me read you extensively from William James.

460
00:57:40,150 --> 00:57:46,690
Now, however fixed these elements of reality may be, we still have a certain freedom in our dealings with them.

461
00:57:47,150 --> 00:57:58,590
Take our sensations, that they are is undoubtedly beyond our control, but which we attend to, note, and make emphatic in our conclusions depends on our own interests

462
00:57:58,590 --> 00:58:05,290
and, according as we lay the emphasis here or there, quite different formulations of truth result.

463
00:58:06,150 --> 00:58:08,070
We read the same facts differently.

464
00:58:08,830 --> 00:58:15,070
Waterloo, with the same fixed detail, spells a victory for an Englishman, for a Frenchman it spells defeat.

465
00:58:15,350 --> 00:58:22,510
So for an optimist philosopher, the universe spells victory for a pessimist, defeat.

466
00:58:22,510 --> 00:58:28,830
What we say about reality thus depends on the perspective into which we throw it.

467
00:58:29,670 --> 00:58:36,490
William James said this back in 1890, in understanding how we co-create our reality.

468
00:58:38,930 --> 00:58:44,230
Here's one of my favorite poems by R.L. Sharpe.

469
00:58:44,910 --> 00:58:54,430
Isn't it strange how princes and kings and clowns that caper in sawdust rings and common people like you and me are builders for eternity.

470
00:58:55,070 --> 00:59:05,650
Each is given a list of rules, a shapeless mass, a bag of tools, and each must fashion your life is flown a stumbling block or a stepping stone.

471
00:59:07,800 --> 00:59:14,180
Very often what we get, the beginning, is a large piece of stone.

472
00:59:15,120 --> 00:59:25,520
What do we do with it? Do we create it into a beautiful David, chipping away the excess stone, or is it a barrier to our success?

473
00:59:26,300 --> 00:59:33,020
And very often it is up to us as we co-create our reality. The stone is there.

474
00:59:33,320 --> 00:59:38,580
We're not responsible for it, but what we do with it, we are responsible for.

475
00:59:47,280 --> 00:59:57,890
Cognitive reconstruction is about learning to interpret things optimistically. In other words, in a positive light.

476
01:00:01,900 --> 01:00:05,540
Ann Harbison, a colleague of mine, used to teach also with Philip Stone,

477
01:00:07,290 --> 01:00:10,290
said that never let a good crisis go to waste.

478
01:00:10,830 --> 01:00:13,030
Crises have the potential for growth.

479
01:00:13,790 --> 01:00:16,610
One of the books that we'll talk about when we talk about relationships

480
01:00:17,410 --> 01:00:23,570
is Passionate Marriage by David Schnark, who talks about crucibles in marriage.

481
01:00:23,570 --> 01:00:33,770
And what he says is that the thriving relationships are relationships not that have been, you know, all fine and dandy for years. These usually fail after a while.

482
01:00:33,930 --> 01:00:45,240
But the relationships that go through crucibles, that have crises, disagreements, and then, you know, thesis, antithesis, and then there is a synthesis.

483
01:00:45,460 --> 01:00:48,580
Then there is growth as a result of these hardships.

484
01:00:48,940 --> 01:00:56,120
And he says that there is no other way. Again, learn to fail or fail to learn. And there is no other way for a long-term success of a relationship.

485
01:00:56,740 --> 01:01:00,060
A relationship does not, cannot look like this.

486
01:01:00,800 --> 01:01:02,840
It has to have the ups and downs.

487
01:01:04,980 --> 01:01:08,000
Never let a good crisis in a relationship go to waste.

488
01:01:08,620 --> 01:01:17,580
That doesn't mean that every crisis needs to be resolved. And there are some that could end and should end a relationship. But most crises can be solved and need to be dealt with.

489
01:01:18,200 --> 01:01:22,760
Same on an individual level. Work by Warren Bennis.

490
01:01:23,540 --> 01:01:43,760
He's from USC, taught for a few years here at the business school, has identified the leading leaders, whether it's young ones, whom he called the geeks, the 30-year-olds, or the old ones, the geezers, he called them.

491
01:01:43,760 --> 01:01:56,120
He has a book called Geeks and Geezers, where he talks about the differences between the two leadership styles, approaches, passions, interests, and there are numerous differences.

492
01:01:56,320 --> 01:02:01,260
For example, you know, the geeks, the young ones, talk about work-life balance.

493
01:02:01,780 --> 01:02:03,960
The geezers don't know what he means by that.

494
01:02:04,520 --> 01:02:10,320
No, it was usually he, a man, worked while the woman took care of the family and the man.

495
01:02:10,900 --> 01:02:22,520
So that was for the geezers, for the older generation. The younger generation, they talk about work-life balance and how they can incorporate all the elements of their lives. There are, of course, many more women in the geeks than the geezers.

496
01:02:22,920 --> 01:02:24,780
Numerous differences among the generation.

497
01:02:26,220 --> 01:02:27,100
One similarity.

498
01:02:28,520 --> 01:02:38,980
All the great leaders, whether they were 30-year-old or 85-years-old, all of them had been through very rough crucibles

499
01:02:38,980 --> 01:02:41,020
and lived to tell the tale.

500
01:02:42,220 --> 01:02:49,720
Now most people go through crucibles but there are certain people who are able to find benefit in these crucibles and grow as a result.

501
01:02:51,080 --> 01:02:57,600
Never let a good crisis go to waste whether it's professionally, whether it's interpersonally whether it's intrapersonally

502
01:02:58,220 --> 01:03:00,100
in terms of what you're experiencing.

503
01:03:04,940 --> 01:03:13,380
But four years ago or five years ago when I was still a resident tutor in Leverett House, we had one of these pre-business events

504
01:03:13,380 --> 01:03:19,160
where we talked about the upcoming year and the recruiting and so on. That particular year was a terrible year.

505
01:03:19,520 --> 01:03:24,520
Goldman Sachs was laying off, wasn't recruiting on campus.

506
01:03:25,080 --> 01:03:29,980
Credit Suisse, First Boston, they were laying off as well.

507
01:03:30,360 --> 01:03:36,300
Deutsche Bank, first time in its history, was laying off employees. It was a really bad market.

508
01:03:37,400 --> 01:03:54,080
And we talked about this. Each of us gave a spiel about the market and what they can do. Very, very sombering, you know, somber presentation. And then one of the students, one of my students, Shane Fitzcoy, was a senior then, was sitting there.

509
01:03:54,080 --> 01:04:10,920
I'd studied positive psychology there, put up his hand and said, Tal, you portrayed a very pessimistic and negative picture, but as the teacher of positive psychology, can you maybe shed some optimism here?

510
01:04:11,600 --> 01:04:14,820
And a few chuckles in the audience and then silence.

511
01:04:17,610 --> 01:04:25,350
And everyone is looking at me and I don't know what to say. And my immediate reaction was to say, well, Shane, things happen for the best.

512
01:04:27,170 --> 01:04:27,650
exactly

513
01:04:30,480 --> 01:04:35,700
and then just before it came out of my mouth I said I thought to myself actually I don't believe that

514
01:04:36,700 --> 01:04:47,570
I don't believe that things happen for the best but while I don't believe that things happen for the best I do believe

515
01:04:47,570 --> 01:04:56,450
that some people are able to make the best of things that happen and there's a very big difference here

516
01:04:57,670 --> 01:04:58,950
because when I say

517
01:04:59,570 --> 01:05:11,150
I'm not happy about what just happened here I don't like it I'd much rather have the market be a good market, where people are hiring, where they'll come to me on a silver platter and offer me a job.

518
01:05:11,150 --> 01:05:13,490
Of course, I would much rather have that.

519
01:05:13,970 --> 01:05:16,370
So things don't happen for the best necessarily.

520
01:05:16,790 --> 01:05:23,110
But there are some people who accept the situation and then are able to make the best of it.

521
01:05:25,480 --> 01:05:34,460
The same with any crisis. Do you think the leaders, whether the 80- and 90-year-olds, invited those crucibles into their lives, wanted these crucibles to happen?

522
01:05:34,540 --> 01:05:43,080
Do you think partners go into a relationship and say, let's look for that crucible. Let's really fight so that we can have a healthy relationship. Of course not.

523
01:05:44,760 --> 01:05:49,860
But when it happens, that's when they make the best of it.

524
01:05:50,860 --> 01:05:53,160
You know, stuff happens.

525
01:05:54,900 --> 01:05:59,780
And we can either let it pollute or make fertilizer out of it.

526
01:06:03,270 --> 01:06:07,130
I want to show you an example, once again, of a leader.

527
01:06:07,550 --> 01:06:11,590
who is a consummate benefit finder.

528
01:06:58,630 --> 01:07:07,090
We are honored to be talking with Nelson Mandela, the former president of South Africa, whose unshakable courage, his faith and integrity

529
01:07:07,090 --> 01:07:18,850
have inspired millions to see what is possible in their own lives. I've had the pleasure, the honor of meeting Mr. Mandela on a couple of occasions before, and I will tell you, to be in his presence is really,

530
01:07:18,950 --> 01:07:21,850
to be in your presence is like being in the presence of royalty

531
01:07:21,850 --> 01:07:31,930
and grace at the same time i would think i wonder how does a man spend 27 years in prison put there

532
01:07:31,930 --> 01:07:39,690
by an oppressor and come out of that experience with not a heart of stone not a cold heart but a

533
01:07:39,690 --> 01:07:45,730
heart that is willing to forgive and embrace i remember talking to you uh one night over dinner

534
01:07:45,730 --> 01:07:52,370
and you had said to me that our hatred for the oppressor was so intense we did not see the value

535
01:07:52,370 --> 01:07:59,730
of talking to him. So at what point did you see the value of letting go of the hatred and begin

536
01:07:59,730 --> 01:08:10,250
the process of talking? Well, let me say first, that is a great tragedy to spend the best of your

537
01:08:10,250 --> 01:08:15,410
lives in prison. But although it looks

538
01:08:15,410 --> 01:08:25,870
ionical, there are advantages in that. If I had not been to prison, I have not, I

539
01:08:25,870 --> 01:08:32,390
would not have been able to achieve the most difficult task in life, and that is

540
01:08:32,390 --> 01:08:38,730
changing yourself. I would not have had that opportunity. I had that opportunity

541
01:08:38,730 --> 01:08:45,570
Because in prison, you have what we don't have in our work outside prison.

542
01:08:47,630 --> 01:08:55,710
The opportunity to sit down and think, which is an important part. Did you need 27 years of it then?

543
01:08:57,090 --> 01:08:57,810
Really?

544
01:08:59,010 --> 01:09:05,130
You could have taken a few days, a week, a vacation. Did you need 27 years?

545
01:09:10,660 --> 01:09:20,770
Now, did it happen for the best that he spent 27 years of his life? Certainly when he experienced it, it was painful, it was awful, but he was able to transform this.

546
01:09:21,910 --> 01:09:31,170
And even in that situation, to see the positive, and when we transformed it, those who know the history of what happened to him, when he started to look at the positive even there,

547
01:09:31,270 --> 01:09:34,350
at the opportunities in prison,

548
01:09:35,450 --> 01:09:42,730
when he changed his mindset, in other words, not the external circumstances, The external circumstances actually began to change.

549
01:09:48,490 --> 01:09:57,210
Another benefit finder, a pessimist sees difficulty in every opportunity, an optimist sees the opportunity in every difficulty.

550
01:09:58,730 --> 01:10:00,590
And you don't want to argue with him.

551
01:10:07,310 --> 01:10:18,010
I want to end by telling you a story, a personal story, about my experiences throughout the year, some biographical details,

552
01:10:18,010 --> 01:10:21,270
Some things that most of you do not know.

553
01:10:22,130 --> 01:10:28,490
And I'm going to tell it to you first from the perspective of the fault finder.

554
01:10:32,890 --> 01:10:35,790
I have a mild case of ADD.

555
01:10:36,750 --> 01:10:42,210
It makes things very difficult for me. It's difficult for me to focus. My mind wanders often.

556
01:10:42,790 --> 01:10:45,210
And I really struggle with it constantly.

557
01:10:49,540 --> 01:10:59,220
When I was in high school, I really wanted to get into a lucrative program in mathematics.

558
01:10:59,720 --> 01:11:04,600
I didn't pass the entry exam. I was very disappointed. I didn't make it.

559
01:11:09,040 --> 01:11:15,560
Becoming a professional squash player was a dream for me since the age of 11.

560
01:11:16,300 --> 01:11:24,500
That's the thing that I thought about constantly. That was the most important thing for me at the time. I wanted to be a professional player.

561
01:11:26,960 --> 01:11:34,200
At the age of 20, I was about to complete my military service in Israel

562
01:11:34,200 --> 01:11:40,600
and got injured on the squash court and pulled a muscle in my back.

563
01:11:40,720 --> 01:11:48,840
The doctor gave me an option of being operated on, which was a big risk,

564
01:11:49,300 --> 01:11:51,260
or giving up my professional career.

565
01:11:52,200 --> 01:11:57,600
I chose to give up my professional career, a dream that I had from a very young age.

566
01:11:59,880 --> 01:12:00,960
It was a real blow.

567
